<%- include('partials/header') %>

<main id="main-content">
  <article>
    <h1>About Me: My Full Story</h1>

    <section class="card" aria-labelledby="early-tech">
      <h2 id="early-tech">How it started</h2>
      <p>
        I am Bassem Krayem. I was born blind on 11 August 2004. My love for
        tech started when I bought my first smartphone, a Galaxy J2. I loved
        exploring everything, every setting in it. I remember breaking a lot of
        things, and since I didn't know how to fix them, I just formatted it
        lol. I remember that one day in 2015 I formatted it three times—or
        let's say I reset it to factory settings three times in one day.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then in 2017 I bought my first laptop, a Dell. At first I used it only
        to watch YouTube, for two and a half years. Later I discovered the
        world of audio games, the gaming world for blind people. At the end of
        2019 and the start of 2020, I started audio gaming. I remember I started
        with <em>The Killer</em> by
        <a href="https://blindmasters.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >blindmasters.org<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a
        >
        lol—they don't run it anymore, it's old now. Then I played the most
        popular online game among blind people all over the world:
        <strong>STW (Survive the Wild)</strong> by
        <a href="https://samtupy.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >samtupy.com<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a
        >
        lol. I kept playing until 2024, when he stopped it. To be honest, all
        the blind people who played it were angry about this decision.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="card" aria-labelledby="becoming-dev">
      <h2 id="becoming-dev">Deciding to be a Developer</h2>
      <p>
        In 2022 I started my journey to become a developer. At first I wanted
        to know how these games are built, but later I said to myself that I
        can't earn money with audio games, so I decided to become a web
        developer. I started reading the HTML tutorial on
        <a href="https://w3schools.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >w3schools.com<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a
        >,
        but I didn't understand it a lot, because it is mostly reading only.
      </p>
      <p>
        By coincidence I discovered the amazing, very amazing—whatever I say
        about this platform, it's not enough—<strong>freeCodeCamp</strong>
        (<a href="https://freecodecamp.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >freecodecamp.org<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a
        >).
        It's the most accessible website; they considered blind people when they
        designed their curriculum. So I learned Responsive Web Design with them.
        Yes, it's true that I am blind, but I was following instructions like
        "create a flex property" or "set the background color"—pure
        instructions I can follow. I learned HTML and CSS, then I learned JS—not
        much, just a little bit.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="card" aria-labelledby="education-struggle">
      <h2 id="education-struggle">The School System &amp; Bakaloria</h2>
      <p>
        I passed my bakaloria exam. I was in the literature section at the
        Ennour School for the Blind in Ben Arous, Tunis. I wanted to be in the
        Informatique section, but at that time, when I was a high school
        student, they didn't allow us. But now blind students can choose
        Informatique! Can you believe it? They are able to do it, but I wasn't,
        because by coincidence some of them met the President of Tunisia and
        told him about the problem, and <strong>booooom</strong>, suddenly it
        became normal!
      </p>
      <p>
        Ok, let's move on with my story. After getting my bakaloria certificate,
        I said, ok, let's try to study software engineering at university. But
        it was hard, because at a normal university you don't study only
        programming and coding concepts; there are a lot of subjects like math
        and physics, so my blindness would not help. So I
        decided to study English at the Faculty of Letters of Manouba. I studied
        the first semester, from October 2023 until January 2024.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="card" aria-labelledby="holberton-test">
      <h2 id="holberton-test">The Holberton Adventure</h2>
      <p>
        Then I discovered
        <a href="https://holbertonschool.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >Holberton School<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a
        >
        in January 2024. I passed the admission test, which had 3 phases. The
        first was answering some questions to test your ability to search—I
        only remember something about the ENIAC computer. In the second phase,
        they gave us a Linux server, and we had to do some tasks like
        installing the Emacs editor and then setting up a static site with
        Apache.
      </p>
      <p>
        The third phase... lol, I remember a funny thing that happened to me. I
        found a page telling me I had 30 minutes to finish, but where is the
        "Start" button, Bassem? I spent a whole day searching for it! I posted
        in a forum, thinking my screen reader was unable to find it. Then the
        girl who told me about Holberton told me they would contact me about
        this stage lol. I was waiting and counting every minute for their call!
        Finally I got the call. I told them I am blind, and they told me:
        <em
          >"No problem, if you reached this stage of the test, then you are able
          to study with us."</em
        >
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="card" aria-labelledby="current-path">
      <h2 id="current-path">Specialization &amp; Future</h2>
      <p>
        I started on February 5th, 2024. We studied Linux, then C, then Python,
        with JS, ES6, and the basics of TypeScript. After the first 9 months, I
        chose the Fullstack Web specialization. I studied for 3 months, then I
        decided to take a break to improve myself.
      </p>
      <p>
        I improved my Python by completing the
        <a
          href="https://www.datacamp.com/completed/statement-of-accomplishment/track/5be69eac6cd4b8bf4c3e3ceca658647d26068750"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >Associate Python Developer<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a
        >
        track, and I completed 61% of the
        <a
          href="https://app.datacamp.com/learn/career-tracks/python-programmer"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >Python Developer Track<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a
        >
        on
        <strong
          ><a href="https://datacamp.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >DataCamp<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a
          ></strong
        >
        during this period. It was a good course. I also studied the amazing
        <a
          href="https://www.udemy.com/course/nodejs-express-mongodb-bootcamp/"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >Node.js course by Jonas Schmedtmann<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a
        >
        on
        <a href="https://udemy.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Udemy<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a>.
        And I earned some certifications from
        <strong
          ><a href="https://freecodecamp.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >freeCodeCamp<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a
          ></strong
        >
        at that time:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a
            href="https://www.freecodecamp.org/certification/bassem_krayem/back-end-development-and-apis"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >Back End Development and APIs Certification<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a
          >
        </li>
        <li>
          <a
            href="https://www.freecodecamp.org/certification/bassem_krayem/relational-database-v8"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >Relational Databases Certification<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a
          >
        </li>
        <li>
          <a
            href="https://www.freecodecamp.org/certification/bassem_krayem/quality-assurance-v7"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >Quality Assurance Certification<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a
          >
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Then I went back to study at Holberton after strengthening myself more,
        and here I am on April 21, 2026. I am building this website right now
        because it's a project at Holberton School—they want us to build a
        website for ourselves. Whenever there are new things in my story, if I
        still have time in this life, I will write them on this page in the
        future,
        <strong>Inshallah</strong>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="how-built">
      <h2 id="how-built">How I built this website</h2>
      <p>
        The GitHub Student Pack includes free services. I got the domain
        <strong>bassem-krayem.dev</strong> free for a year from the Name.com
        website, and I host it on Heroku, also for free thanks to the GitHub
        Student Pack.
      </p>
      <p>
        I built it with Node.js and Express, server-side rendered with the EJS
        templating engine. You can find the repo of this project on my
        <a
          href="https://github.com/bassem-krayem/bassem-krayem-website"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >GitHub<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a
        >
        if you want to take a look at its simple code.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="skills-tech">
      <h2 id="skills-tech">Technical Skills &amp; Technologies</h2>
      <p>
        In my journey through Holberton and self-study, I have worked with these
        tools:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Languages:</strong> Python, Node.js (JavaScript/ES6), Bash
          scripting, and the basics of TypeScript.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Databases &amp; DBMS:</strong> MongoDB with Mongoose, PostgreSQL
          (PSQL), and MySQL.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Frameworks &amp; Tools:</strong> Express.js, simple knowledge of
          Flask, Linux (Ubuntu), Git, and GitHub.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Specialties:</strong> Backend Architecture, RESTful APIs, and
          Accessibility (A11y).
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="human-languages">
      <h2 id="human-languages">Languages I Speak</h2>
      <p>
        I am a bilingual engineer: <strong>Arabic</strong> (Native) and
        <strong>English</strong> (Intermediate).
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="hobbies">
      <h2 id="hobbies">Beyond the Code</h2>
      <p>When I am not sitting in front of my laptop, I am usually:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Reading:</strong> I have a deep passion for novels. I love
          exploring electronic books through the <strong>Abjad app</strong>, the
          <strong>Motamayezoon library</strong>, or various Telegram channels. I
          also enjoy following stories on <strong>Wattpad</strong>. Reading is
          my way of traveling the world through my imagination.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Sports:</strong> I love exercising. It keeps my mind sharp
          and my body active after long hours of coding.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Audio Gaming:</strong> Though I spend less time on it now, I
          still enjoy the community and the challenge of well-designed audio
          games.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </article>
</main>

